 be seeing more  prostate cancer and yet the incidence  rates plunged and this was really  because of  profound drops in psa early detection  programs  after first the 2018 and then 2012  recommendations from the uspstf  mortality rates in the meantime where we  have data going back to the 1930s  had really been very slowly rising  throughout the  20th century i'm really peaking around  1990  to 1995 or so and then have been falling  pretty progressively over a 20-year  period  at the fastest rate of any cancer except  for lung cancer  now we know this decline in lung cancer  is attributable almost entirely to  smoking cessation programs  the decline in prostate cancer is a  little bit controversial but there's  really little argument that at least  30 to 50 percent of this decline is  directly attributable to early detection  programs  and it's concerning that in the most  recent years of the data we actually see  the mortality decline  stabilizing or perhaps even reversing  for the first time  this is not entirely due to the drop in  screening but there's no question  that as mortality rates increase if we  continue to drop down in terms of  screening we will see  more prostate cancer mortality now why  is this important for disparity well  over all these years  that we have seen the ups and downs of  incidents and the decline in mortality  we have very consistently year after  year seen a very substantial  racial disparity whereby  african-american men compared to  caucasian men  have consistently born about a two and a  half fold increased burden  not just of prostate cancer diagnosis  but of diagnosis with metastatic and  ultimately lethal  disease and this has really not changed  much year after year decade after decade  from 1970s  uh to the mid-2000s this rate ratio  varied up and down a little bit but  really never  fell substantially even as rates as  mortality rates did  fall among african-american men and  caucasian men the gap really has not  narrowed  very much now as many things in  epidemiology the  the devil is often in the details and  when we drill down  below the national level we actually see  pretty profound variation in these  trends over time  this was a nice study looking at 50  different  urban areas across the u.s comparing  mortality rates from 1990s  to the late 2000s and what this is  looking at  is the change over time for caucasian  men  in the white circles here and  african-american men in the black  circles here  and looking at the delta delta the  difference over time so for example in  san francisco  my city uh there's this is a fairly  typical case where there was about a 44  drop in mortality for for white men 43  drop for black men  so there's a comparable decline but  really no change  in the delta no change in the gap  between between the groups  over the years but you can see some  extreme counter examples in minnesota  for example in minneapolis the mortality  rate fell about 30  for caucasians over 70 percent of  african-americans so here there's a  pretty substantial  narrowing of the gap at the other  extreme in oklahoma city  mortality rates fell about 40 for  caucasians and actually rose 8  for african-american men so substantial  widening of the gap and of course a lot  of this is random variation this is not  you know nobody's saying that there are  specific programs that we can point to  in one city or another  which explain this a lot of this is  statistical noise  but it's still important to really  understand the local patterns  um of where everyone lives and practices  in terms of trying to understand what is  going on in the local populations  now there's a paper that came out just a  couple months ago  again looking at seer data which  suggests  that in the most recent years we may be  seeing a little bit of progress so this  is the  mid-2000s up through the mid 2010s  showing that this rate ratio has been  declining over this  time period from about three to around  2.5  for men 50 to  and for older men we've seen a similar  decline  uh now this may be this may be a true  decline although if you think back to  the graph that i showed earlier the rate  ratio has varied over the years between  two and three so this may just be a  little bit more you know natural  variation  within the consistent range that we've  seen over the decades  now to an extent it seems to be  the case that prostate cancer actually  follows a similar trajectory among  african-american men  as it does among white men but that it  starts about five years earlier  this is a paper from a set of models run  by the cisnet group  this is a group of statistical modelers  funded by the nci  and the the prostate working group who  have been really focused on  nothing but understanding the psa  observed incidence and mortality data  through the lens of screening programs  for nearly 30 years now it's  investigators from  the freight hutch in seattle led by ruth  ozioni by a group in michigan and  uh group in erasmus in rotterdam who  have all been  looking at the observed data through  different mathematical models  and all three models gave a fairly  consistent  output a consistent story that again the  trajectory may be similar but the  onset of prostate cancer the first  growth of the cells and ultimately the  clinical presentation  appears to be about five years earlier  on average  for black men compared to white men and  we see this if we look  at the again look at the the rate ratio  data the increased burden borne by black  men compared to  white men this is looking across time  now  not over time we see that by far the  highest rate ratios are seen for the  younger  men okay so for men in their late 40s  african-american men actually have a  four-fold higher  likelihood of being diagnosed with  metastatic disease compared to caucasian  men  and as men get older the gap narrows and  ultimately flattening out around  a two-fold higher rate of metastatic  disease for men by the time they get to  their 80s  again suggesting that a large  part of the explanation for the excess  burden of disease borne by black men may  be seen in the fact that the disease  starts  earlier and it's really younger  african-american men who are in fact  at greatest excess risk  so nothing that i've said so far is  really at all controversial it's been  widely recognized for  a long time that prostate cancer is  worse among african-american men  african-american men get more prostate  cancer they get more high-risk disease  and ultimately more  lethal disease but none of the data that  none of the studies that i've shown have  really gotten at the important question  which is why  is this cell and there's a nice  framework for  thinking about what health their health  disparity research should look like  which proposes three phases of disparity  research  phase one is identifying that a  disparity exists phase two is  elucidating the underlying causes of the  disparity  and phase three is developing  interventions to try to ameliorate this  disparity  and the fact is we've really been stuck  in phase one pretty much forever  in prostate cancer we are just starting  to dip our toes into phase two  and really have not begun to scratch  what what should ultimately be phase  three so you know what are at the roots  what what can we say in phase two  that that can potentially explain what  we know to be  a very substantial disparity well the  obvious uh potential sources  of of explanation here are genetic  factors environmental factors  and then structural factors which are  basically access to care and the quality  of care delivered now of course there's  multiple  sub levels of investigation here in  terms of environmental factors well it's  an incredibly broad category that  is things like diet smoking allostatic  stressors it's also things like  neighborhood factors  and structural bias within the  healthcare delivery system kind of falls  under