e for men by the time they get to  their 80s  again suggesting that a large  part of the explanation for the excess  burden of disease borne by black men may  be seen in the fact that the disease  starts  earlier and it's really younger  african-american men who are in fact  at greatest excess risk  so nothing that i've said so far is  really at all controversial it's been  widely recognized for  a long time that prostate cancer is  worse among african-american men  african-american men get more prostate  cancer they get more high-risk disease  and ultimately more  lethal disease but none of the data that  none of the studies that i've shown have  really gotten at the important question  which is why  is this cell and there's a nice  framework for  thinking about what health their health  disparity research should look like  which proposes three phases of disparity  research  phase one is identifying that a  disparity exists phase two is  elucidating the underlying causes of the  disparity  and phase three is developing  interventions to try to ameliorate this  disparity  and the fact is we've really been stuck  in phase one pretty much forever  in prostate cancer we are just starting  to dip our toes into phase two  and really have not begun to scratch  what what should ultimately be phase  three so you know what are at the roots  what what can we say in phase two  that that can potentially explain what  we know to be  a very substantial disparity well the  obvious uh potential sources  of of explanation here are genetic  factors environmental factors  and then structural factors which are  basically access to care and the quality  of care delivered now of course there's  multiple  sub levels of investigation here in  terms of environmental factors well it's  an incredibly broad category that  is things like diet smoking allostatic  stressors it's also things like  neighborhood factors  and structural bias within the  healthcare delivery system kind of falls  under