thing fundamentally different about  the biology of prostate cancer among  black compared to white men there are  some differences here  but they are you know they're relatively  subtle and none of these again would  suggest that we are dealing with  fundamentally a different biology  so there may be an answer to be found in  screening  if you look at the screening guidelines  in 2020  we do see a gradual convergence  over the you know or beyond what has  been  a very controversial and divergent set  of guidelines over the years so the us  preventive services task force which i  mentioned earlier  is by far the most influential body when  it comes to driving  screening decisions and screening  recommendations by primary care  providers to their patients  so in 2012 the uspstf recommended that  nobody should be screened ever  regardless of race or family history or  any of the risk factors  now as of 2018 they recommend shared  decision making for men  55 to 69 and they recommend against  four men over 70. they have no specific  recommendations for african-american men  there's some text in there calling for  uh more research although the prospect  of getting a randomized screening trials  specifically focused on african-american  men  is extremely low and even if such a  study happened we would not get results  for  20 more years and this 50 to 5 to 69  age range here is by the way is not  based on  any evidence to suggest that that is the  optimal time to screen the reason that  age range is recommended  is that there's really only one large  multi-center randomized trial  which actually addresses the question of  screening versus no screening it's the  erspc  a large multinational european trial  which has read out its results over the  years and  in that trial the core age range for  randomization was 55 to 69. so that is  the reason that this  age is is the basis for the uspstf  us psts recommendation it's almost  certainly not optimal there was another  trial which included men 50 to 55 which  actually had better results than the  rspc which was not included here  and it is very likely the case that  screening earlier  makes the most sense so the aua the  american neurological association  now is actually relatively similar to  the uspsdf  in also recommending shared  decision-making for men in the same core  age range 55-70  they don't really make any  recommendations for younger men they  recommend against for older men and  again no specific recommendations for  african-american men  now the other guidelines the nccn and  the american cancer society  are both based on a broader  consideration of literature rather than  focusing just on this  on the randomized trials they recommend  shared decision making again  uh for a broader age range the nccn  recommends for men 45 to 75  acs recommends starting at 50. acs  explicitly recommends starting about  five years earlier if there are risk  factors even 10 years earlier if there's  multiple risk factors including  african-american race so african  americans with family history where you  start to get multiple risk factors this  is where you really want to think about  starting at 40.  the nccn is a little bit less crisp but  they do recommend  starting screenings several years  earlier again for african american  men so what about access you know what  do we actually  see in terms of screening patterns for  black compared to white men  this may actually be a little bit of  good news and these studies have been  done repeatedly over the years you know  the fundamental message here is is still  not great news if you ignore disparity  for a minute which is that the most  common age for screening in america  is still late 60s and early 70s and this  is not the right time to do  screening with psa men by this age tend  to have bph  they tend to have many competing sources  of psa and this is an age where we see a  lot of  over detection of low risk disease under  detection  of high risk disease and ultimately over  treatment  and under treatment as well by far the  best time to use psa is in  late 40s early 50s maybe even up to age  60 where psa with a lower threshold is  much more specific  for prostate cancer specifically and  this may actually be  a bit of good news within this overall  you know problematic screening pattern  which is that black men are actually  more likely to be screened at younger  ages than white men a little bit less  likely to be screened  than white men at older ages so this is  less under screening for younger men  and actually less over screening for  older men so  so this is actually likely a favorable  trend i think reflecting an  understanding over the years that  black men are at higher risk and should  be potentially screened  earlier the problem though is that this  20 30  right here is still much too low and if  we want to make a difference  we probably need to drive these rates up  specifically in the younger age ranges  quite substantially and there is a  rationale to think that  a large portion of the disparity could  be ameliorated with a more aggressive  early detection program directed  specifically  at african american men it's important  to note that when we talk about early  screening  we need to forget all about 4.0 as the  threshold  to drive further evaluations so  4.0 was chosen because one psa was  developed as a screening test  most men in the screening studies were  in their 60s just as  in the study here and the fact is for  younger men for men  even up to age 60 the median is around  1.0 for men in their 40s and 50s  the median is 0.7 or 0.8 and this has  been shown very consistently across  multiple studies  and multiple ethnicities multiple  countries  so the median for men in their 40s  is around 0.6 0.7 in your early 50s  around 0.8 by the time you get to 60  around 1.0 and again this is identical  for white men black men there's a large  study out of japan which again shows  exactly the same  medium psas so what this says  is that with a single psa test before  age 60 we can pretty much  rule out prostate cancer for at least  half the population and probably even  more  these studies summarized here looked at  long-term outcomes after a single  baseline psa going out to  10 20 30 years and found that this  single baseline test was highly highly  predictive  so men under the median or even under  the 75th percentile in many cases can  basically  not worry about prostate cancer but men  above median basically above 1.0 is a  good way to remember it  need to at least think about workup with  secondary tests  not necessarily with biopsy but either  with  with more careful monitoring going  forward or with  workup now what about treatment  do we have evidence of any sort of  bias or discrepancy in terms of access  to high quality treatment  well this is a look at the likelihood of  receiving definitive treatment as  opposed to  no definitive treatment and what we see  is  over the years here from 2004 up to 2013  higher rates of expectant management or  no definitive treatment for  african-american compared to  caucasian men in a large study out of  the national cancer data bank  and now the ncdb is focused really in  hospital  based care so this is going to  under-capture  active surveillance done in the  community setting  and this is not particularly  risk-adjusted so it's a little bit hard  to know but this  is suggestive perhaps of more  under-treatment among african-american  men  there was a study reported last year  using  a new seer sub-database focused on  watchful weighting  which looked at the odds of receiving  active surveillance for  risk prostate cancer for black versus  other men they found variation from year  to year  suggesting in 2008 they concluded in  2015 there was a drop in the use of  surveillance for black men but this was  really an  insufficiently adjusted study and  and a follow-up paper which is going to  be coming out in press  relatively soon shows that with further  adjustment  for other factors like income education  access to health care providers in the  community specific geographic location  black versus white race drops out  completely the hazard ratio here is 0.99  there is really not a hint of a  difference between the two groups if  anything it's actually hispanic men who  have a lower risk  of acting a lower likelihood of active  surveillance to watch for waiting  compared to the other groups  and the other story here is that any  sort of racial  difference is really swamped by just raw  variation this is  looking at the different seer registries  registry by registry  and within the registry is the  individual counties and the county level  variation really swamps everything  and we've known this to be a problem for  many many years in prostate cancer care  and health were in the health care  system  you know what you get depends far too  much on whose door you happen to knock  on in terms of which which doctor you  wind up seeing  and getting at this variation problem  certainly has to be part of the project  in terms of ameliorating  disparity at the level of race and  so the prior analysis raises the  question is the disparity really driven  by race or  is race just reflecting uh poor social  economic  status reflecting structural racism etc  but basically is an economic problem  rather than  a race problem per se  for one look at this we looked years ago  at the san francisco general hospital  population which is the safety net  hospital serving san francisco county  compared to the university practice at  ucsf  and compared to a large registry called  capture which tracked men diagnosed with  prostate cancer  across 25 urology practices across the  united states  and this is a look at the the risk  profile so zero through nine here is  what we call the capri score  low intermediate and high risk prostate  cancers and over here on the right is  advanced disease at the time of  diagnosis  and in short the men presenting at sf  general hospital had a three-fold higher  rate of presenting with events disease  than those  in in the capture database and much  higher likelihoods of intermediate and  high-risk disease despite the average  age being about the same  at the general and the screening rates  psa diagnosis being  being fairly similar but this was true  in a very  mixed race population so the  sf general population certainly has more  black men  than capture or the broader ucsf  population but we saw this  across the asians across the caucasian  patients in this hospital  hispanics it was really seen across all  the different race groups this this high  prevalence  of high risk and advanced disease  now it's also worth stepping back away  from the united states for a minute in  terms of trying to gain some  insights from global epidemiology in  terms of prostate cancer because  prostate cancer is increasingly a global  disease  we used to think of this as a disease  really just affecting men in the  developed world  who would live long enough to not die  from infectious diseases or violent  violent causes malnutrition etc but as  the world is developing it is  more and more of a concern  internationally prostate cancer  is now one of the higher causes  of incidence internationally almost 10  million cases a year  anticipated and it has now passed  esophageal cancer to be the fifth  leading cause of cancer mortality around  the world  and but of course incidence varies  dramatically with geographic  locale now what's interesting is if you  look at the  incidence data they really do tend to  track with screening rates so  the developed world tends to have higher  rates of incidence although there are  certainly hot spots in the caribbean for  example in brazil  south africa where we see exceptions  here but what's interesting is that the  incidence data and the mortality data  have relatively little  association with each other so there are  some countries  sweden for example which screens heavily  but still has a relatively high  mortality rate  the story in the u.s is fairly typical  of what we would hope to see with a  screening program which is heavy  screening  tends to drive down mortality rates  and then if we look for the highest  mortality rates for prostate cancer in  the world  we find them in areas with relatively  little screenings specifically in  sub-saharan and west africa  and in the caribbean we see high  incidence and  mortality and if we look at the highest  mortality rates in the world many of  these countries  are in the caribbean so it's the  combination  we think of countries where there's a  high population of west african origin  and relative economic development  compared to  the actual west african countries so the  men have the opportunity to live long  enough  uh to to potentially get lethal prostate  cancer  and this is really where we're seeing  these these rates which are  truly off the chart if you compare them  to the u.s where the age standardized  mortality rate is is about 7.7  we're seeing rates as high as seven  times uh  that rate when we look at some of these  jamaican some of these caribbean  countries and again the  there is a little bearing here little  association consistently between  development index  um and mortality rates  uh this is just one more look at the at  this association or poor association  lack of association between incidents  and mortality  so we know that environmental factors  bear heavily on  prostate cancer incidence and mortality  prostate cancer has been associated  quite clearly with smoking rates  with certain poor dietary habits  high inflammatory diet high fat diets  have been associated with prostate  cancer  there's also the question about stress  and cancer there's there's very poor  or weak data here for prostate cancer  specifically  but there have been uh some at least in  vitro studies  in mice and ce